Batch file edits: was unexpected at this time
A Windows script stops with, for example, ) was unexpected at this time or . was unexpected at this time. In a .bat file this means a parenthesis inside an if ( ... ) block that the command interpreter took as the end of the block; text in echo lines is the usual place. The shipped scripts avoid this, so the message points at an edit. Escape parentheses inside blocks with ^:
if not exist "%FILE%" (
  echo Missing %FILE% ^(see the example file^).
  exit /b 1
)
